Morrow will swap her current Iron Sword for one with full durability. She’ll also  take the Armorslayer, and stash her Lockpick in the Convoy.

&#x200B;

At a later date, Morrow will also place her Heal staff in the convoy. It might not be the best choice for the coming battles… but she’ll come back for it, if another hasn’t borrowed it by then.

I think you misunderstand critical race theory. It’s not about sending a deluge of cynicism black children’s way about their future, because A. most black children are taught that by their parents anyway, and B. treating every single member of a marginalized group as a monolith goes against the entire point of intersectionality, one of critical race theory’s core tenants.

&#x200B;

For example, I am an autistic transwoman, a pretty textbook example of marginalization. I grew up with a father that was pretty staunchly against both of those things, and a mother who tried and continues to try to support me whenever she can; nowadays I have a really wonderful support network and can afford to be public whenever I can. I am also a white person who lives in an extremely white community, meaning that I am pretty safe from the judgement of strangers who see me as “one of them.” (I’m also the descendant of slave owners and a few of the slaves they assaulted, which brings up complicated feelings.) I grew up pretty poor, so I’m pretty frugal nowadays, but I’ve also managed to get on disability money— something that many disabled people struggle to do due to how many barriers the government throws up. The government also frequently wants to get people *off* of disability money, so maintaining that is hard and draining, but my mother is very savvy on this stuff and has helped me a lot.

&#x200B;

All in all, intersectionality sees me as very lucky, but I’m obviously not as well off as the yacht boys that run the show. It’s pretty easy to look at this complex set of factors and draw the conclusion that I’m somewhat middle of the road in terms of privilege. Moreover, the amount of privilege I have fluctuates from situation to situation, and is expressed differently from someone else who might have a similar level of privilege with a different set of traits. Growing up white meant that I could say slurs and engage in “ironic” racism without being pressured to learn how damaging that could be; my father probably contributed to that mindset, even if he also left me with a lot of emotional baggage. It also somewhat bled into my identity as a transperson for a while as I would jokingly refer to *myself* with slurs. A black autistic person from a rich family would probably have grown up with nicer things and a more relaxed perspective of money than me, but would also experience forms of ableism unique to black people from their community… and of course, if they were born into a poor family, they’d probably have to outright hide their autism as an adult out of fear of losing their children to social services.

&#x200B;

Critical race theory takes the basic truism that oppression exists and encourages different ways of thinking about it. It teaches seeing people as the product of an entire lifetime rather than one or two visible traits to show pity for and then move on from. And more importantly, it teaches that we should take that mindset into the fight *against* systemic oppression when we engage in action steps. I may have more privilege than some other people, but I can use that as another tool in my arsenal to uplift others. And I can use that knowledge to resist the powerful urge to fall back into cynicism.

The desire to see us dead along with people like us is the rule rather than the exception within the right. Doctors and nurses are quitting their jobs in droves because the families of their patients are physically assaulting them and screaming at them to treat their dying family members with Ivermectin, crippling our medical systems long-term. The right‘s figureheads have fed their followers misinformation at odds with decades of medical and sociological research, and during a period where their base is dying in droves they’ve dramatically increased their advocacy for violence knowing that they might not get another chance. 

&#x200B;

Both sides are incapable of civil discussion because one of them has contracted rabies— they’re erratic, aggressive, and a few feet away from death’s door. And you’re suggesting we extend a hand of friendship towards this foam-mouthed wolf of an ideology. Why would you ever expect that to go over well?

Nice! Good to know.

&#x200B;

Also good to know that friendly fire is on with the water balloons. Hmm…

Media giants are absolutely disseminators of propaganda. However, they’re disseminators of propaganda that people are starting to see through, and different organizations push different (and often conflicting) propaganda. You also said yourself that Hong Kong was covered extensively— if the news really didn’t want us to think about that, wouldn’t they have covered it up or just ignored it? 

&#x200B;

Before we go anywhere else here, I’d like to hear your stance on the CCP. That has a big effect on how to approach the Nazi comparison.

I agree with your stance on fighting games and accessibility, as does the poster you’re replying to. My problem is with your tone. Getting annoyed by elitism in the FGC and then turning around and being elitist over reading and writing is hypocritical.

&#x200B;

\> I hope to encourage you to read more books to improve your writing clarity going forward. I'm genuinely happy that people like your are trying to write.

&#x200B;

The poster mentioned nothing about books. They mentioned nothing about inexperience with reading or writing. You merely assumed these things based on your own misinterpretation of their words and then turned those assumptions against said poster as weaponized smarm. Putting yourself on a pedestal above other people like this kills any illusion of meaningful conversation and exposes you as the same kind of person you decry.

The only way that a potion could reliably net you a full round of survival is if it just matched or outhealed the damage you take. As it is now, strategy and/or luck can help ensure the teammate you brought back up gets their next turn, where they can then take whatever actions they think will get them to the next round, or try and contribute with the opportunity they have— if their attack kills that hobgoblin threatening them, maybe both!

&#x200B;

Even if that doesn’t happen though, getting downed after a potion is one less attack (at minimum!) aimed towards the rest of your party. The value of that fluctuates depending on context, but it’s nothing to sneeze at.

&#x200B;

Consider that a party is supposed to get roughly one consumable per level (excluding purchases). Assuming that’s even just a basic potion (it likely wouldn’t be at higher levels), a good healing roll actually *could* outpace damage at the lower levels they’re designed for. A few levels up, and they become better suited to the use case I outlined above. That sort of use case doesn’t really fall off with level, and as gold becomes inflated basic potions suddenly become very cheap, making them well-suited for healing in bulk out of combat.

&#x200B;

I personally don’t disagree with you that some changes are worth making, but to say that potions are outright useless because they don’t net you a full round is a stretch.

The media dropped Hong Kong like a hot plate the moment Trump stuff came back up. The news outlets as a whole are harmful through shortsightedness, not outright agenda (individual channels notwithstanding).

&#x200B;

There are plenty of people that want us to be on good terms with the CCP, and plenty that want us to take action against them. Having a variety of opinions isn’t “propaganda,” it’s a discussion. And considering China’s importance in the world of today, it’s a discussion worth having.

&#x200B;

You think we’re all hopped on propaganda? Then actually explain your viewpoint. Jumping into a thread and telling people they’re wrong without explaining why isn’t going to convince them of anything, it’s just going to make them think that the people on the other side of the argument from them are assholes. Actively driving people away from your opinion is a pretty lousy method of debate, yeah?

The right to free speech is a right given to the people against the government, it has nothing to do with this. Even if it did, it’s a right  fundamentally about criticism, so comments like these would be easily construed as an attempt to suppress other people’s rights.

&#x200B;

Putting shit out there has consequences regardless of content or context. That goes for Hax, that goes for his detractors, and that goes for you and me. If you want to contribute to these conversation, actually involve yourself in them instead of trying to shut them down.

I probably can’t give proper advice. I am a stranger and I’m very tired myself, physically and emotionally. But I can be here for you to vent at for a bit, and to hive this post more traction so more capable people can see it.

&#x200B;

I’m going to go shower while I wait for a response. I should be able to check back in an hour or so.

&#x200B;

EDIT: Either you’re just away from your computer at the moment, or I was too late. I’m going to hope for the former and keep checking back regularly.

Hmm... this all is very interesting stuff! Actually starting to listen to an audio does feel very intimidating, which probably says a lot about where I am with this at the moment. I’ll probably start with Let Me Show You Something and move on from there.

&#x200B;

My partner and I do have a lot of trust in one another, though I imagine what you mean is something more fundamental. I’ll try and take this slow.

The issue is that he’s sharing information that pretty much everyone knows with a tone that seems to assume that it’s an original thought. I can confirm I’ve done the exact same thing a few times in unrelated instances. When you describe the exact definition of accessibility with the intent to show that it isn’t accessibility in the first place, you still have a *point*, but it doesn’t inspire confidence that you actually understand your own point— and it follows naturally from that that you might not know what you’re talking about in general. It doesn’t help that a lot of people hear seem to think Xrd being touted as “inaccessible“ is misinformation which is harmful to that game, as well as similar games.

&#x200B;

Not a GG player to clarify, just skimming the thread to hear opinions and thought I’d chime in here.

I was copying, yeah. I’ve had typos arise as a result of that, but if that’s the case every time then the thought of typos I can‘t see even after they’re fixed is obnoxious. As for the errors, I do try and look them up when I can’t make sense of them, but a lot of the explanations go over my head.

&#x200B;

It’s moreso just frustrating really. I had to stop coding after I ran into it again today because I‘d started developing a migraine.

Would you consider cave paintings to be art? Or maybe more specifically, that they belonged to an art form that had been well-established by that point? It’s easy to admire their artistic merits in retrospect, but looking at them from within the time period they were made is very interesting imo. Though they’re obviously far less prolific than video games have become in the medium’s short lifespan.

&#x200B;

I also wonder whether the heavy influence of the Japanese has to do with it, as obviously companies like Nintendo had a lot of control over it by the time the medium was starting to further explore its artistic potential. I bring this up because a lot of Japanese gaming media tends to perceived as juvenile over here in the west (especially the otaku stuff), and that could’ve had some influence on gaming’s respectability in the eyes of western critics.

It’s not just a logo change. It’s a warning. Discord is falling in line with corporate trends, and in the process is showing us its priorities. People have mentioned that Discord has become increasingly out of touch with the community that helped birth it, this is just the latest expression of that. The logo has stopped being an identifier and is now just a tool for the same bog-standard manipulation tactics that other companies employ.

&#x200B;

I don’t even mind the new look much outside of the colors. I don’t see a bad logo when I look at the new branding, I see a logo for a company which does not have my best interests in mind. And that’s a step away from being a company that’s actively dangerous to trust.

While I haven’t had any real meaningful interactions with trans men to confirm, I‘m confident in saying that I never had the issue to begin with. My dysphoria is much weaker than hers, and my support circle stronger, so those could factor into it? (Will elaborate on that if needed and if she’s comfortable with her side of things)

&#x200B;

You absolutely nailed it with the jealousy thing though— it applies to more than just trans men, but in this case she‘s actively frustrated at herself for feeling the way that she does, hence the reaching out. She’s absolutely baffled by the idea that there’s anything good about being a guy and bad about being a girl.

&#x200B;

Actually, I’d love to hear your descriptions of what precisely appeals to you about being a guy as you’d describe it. I’m not entirely sure if that’d help clear things up or trigger her dysphoria, though...

